On Fri, Mar 28, 2014 at 7:52 AM, Pankaj Agarwal<br>&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel="nofollow">pankaj.ag...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br>&gt; The perl code in my knitr/R script that runs the external tool called<br>&gt; bowtie2:<br>&gt;<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt; ....<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt; ```{r test-perl, engine=&#39;perl&#39;}<br>&gt; use strict;<br>&gt; use warnings;<br>&gt; use Cwd qw(cwd);<br>&gt; my $cur_dir = cwd;<br>&gt; print &quot;
Current working dir: [$cur_dir]

&quot;;<br>&gt; my $bowtie2_exe = &#39;/data/installs/bowtie2/bowtie2-2.2.1/bowtie2&#39;;<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt; my $cmd=&quot;$bowtie2_exe -x $bowtie2_index -1 $fastq_file_pe_1 -2<br>&gt; $fastq_file_pe_2 -S $cur_dir/&quot;.&quot;$fastq_files[$cnt]&quot;.&quot;.sam -p 8&quot;;<br>&gt; print &quot;
command is [$cmd]
&quot;;<br>&gt; system($cmd);<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt;<br>&gt; The variables are populated earlier in the code.  The translated command as<br>&gt; reported in the knitr report text file is:<br>&gt;<br>&gt; ## command is [/data/installs/bowtie2/bowtie2-2.2.1/bowtie2 -x<br>&gt; /data/ref_genomes/human/37.2/Homo_sapiens/NCBI/build37.2/Sequence/Bowtie2Index/genome<br>&gt; -1 /data/projects/knitr/xxxx_1.clipped.fastq.gz -2<br>&gt; /data/projects/knitr/xxxx_2.clipped.fastq.gz -S<br>&gt; /data/projects/knitr/xxxx.sam -p 8]<br>&gt;<br>&gt; When the command is done running, the output from the command is printed out<br>&gt; in the R console:<br>&gt;<br>&gt; 53545457 reads; of these:<br>&gt;   53545457 (100.00%) were paired; of these:<br>&gt;     6637517 (12.40%) aligned concordantly 0 times<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt; ...<br>&gt;<br>&gt; But this is not captured in the knitr report text file.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Ideally it is important to capture this because a change in the parameters<br>&gt; to the bowtie2 command will generate a different output, which should be<br>&gt; captured in the report.  Otherwise I would have to copy and paste it in the<br>&gt; report.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Please let me know if you need further information.<br>&gt;<br>&gt; Thanks,<br>&gt;<br>&gt; - Pankaj<br>
